1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,793
Four observations, two features each.

2
00:00:04,043 --> 00:00:07,837
Call them x and y.

3
00:00:08,087 --> 00:00:14,157
The cloud leans up and to the right.

4
00:00:14,407 --> 00:00:21,995
When x is large,
y tends to be large too.

5
00:00:22,245 --> 00:00:29,833
Can we find a direction
along which they vary most?

6
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:34,635
That's the whole engine of PCA.

7
00:00:34,885 --> 00:00:39,438
We'll build the answer
from scratch.

8
00:00:39,688 --> 00:00:45,000
No formulas until they're
forced on us.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,614
Meet the data:
(5,3), (1,1), (4,4), (2,0).

10
00:00:50,864 --> 00:00:56,479
Average x: 12 / 4 = 3.

11
00:00:56,729 --> 00:01:02,343
Average y: 8 / 4 = 2.

12
00:01:02,593 --> 00:01:09,812
μ = (3, 2) — the mean, in red.

13
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:18,083
We care how the cloud spreads,
not where it sits.

14
00:01:18,333 --> 00:01:23,145
Subtract the mean
from every point.

15
00:01:23,395 --> 00:01:29,010
Watch the cloud slide
to the origin.

16
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:33,270
Centred: (2,1), (−2,−1), (1,2), (−1,−2).

17
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:39,135
Same shape — measured
from the middle.

18
00:01:39,385 --> 00:01:44,999
From here on: deviations
from the mean.

19
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,721
How spread out are the points?

20
00:01:49,971 --> 00:01:53,905
Start with one variable:
variance.

21
00:01:54,155 --> 00:01:58,877
Subtract the mean,
square each deviation.

22
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:05,422
Squaring kills signs —
big gaps count more.

23
00:02:05,672 --> 00:02:12,754
Add them up,
divide by n − 1 (sample).

24
00:02:13,004 --> 00:02:17,725
Population variance
divides by n instead.

25
00:02:17,975 --> 00:02:21,909
Var(x) = Var(y) = 10/3.

26
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:28,454
Variance sees only
one axis at a time.

27
00:02:28,704 --> 00:02:35,000
It can't tell x and y
rise together.

28
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,296
Covariance measures
moving together.

29
00:02:39,546 --> 00:02:44,916
Positive — both rise together.

30
00:02:45,166 --> 00:02:53,759
Negative — one rises
as the other falls.

31
00:02:54,009 --> 00:03:01,527
Near zero — no clear
linear link.

32
00:03:01,777 --> 00:03:07,148
Multiply each point's
two deviations.

33
00:03:07,398 --> 00:03:14,916
2, 2, 2, 2 — all positive.

34
00:03:15,166 --> 00:03:21,611
Cov(x, y) = 8/3 ≈ 2.67.

35
00:03:21,861 --> 00:03:27,231
Var(x) = Var(y) = 10/3.

36
00:03:27,481 --> 00:03:35,000
These three numbers build
the matrix next.

37
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,648
Take one centred point
as a column vector.

38
00:03:40,898 --> 00:03:47,252
Multiply it by its transpose —
an outer product.

39
00:03:47,502 --> 00:03:51,738
x′x′ᵀ = [[x², xy], [xy, y²]]

40
00:03:51,988 --> 00:03:56,224
The dot product gives
one number.

41
00:03:56,474 --> 00:04:01,416
Do it for every point,
then add.

42
00:04:01,666 --> 00:04:06,608
(2,1) and (−2,−1)
give the same matrix.

43
00:04:06,858 --> 00:04:11,800
(1,2) and (−1,−2)
give the same too.

44
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:16,287
Entry sums: 10, 8, 8, 10.

45
00:04:16,537 --> 00:04:20,067
That's the scatter matrix S.

46
00:04:20,317 --> 00:04:24,553
Top-left: x spread.
Bottom-right: y spread.

47
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:26,921
Off-diagonal: joint variation.

48
00:04:27,171 --> 00:04:34,937
C = S / (n − 1) = S / 3.

49
00:04:35,187 --> 00:04:39,423
C = [[10/3, 8/3],
      [8/3, 10/3]]

50
00:04:39,673 --> 00:04:44,615
S and C carry
the same directions.

51
00:04:44,865 --> 00:04:49,101
Dividing by 3 only
shrinks eigenvalues.

52
00:04:49,351 --> 00:04:54,999
C is symmetric —
both cross entries match.

53
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,863
Pick any direction — call it u.

54
00:05:02,113 --> 00:05:07,015
Project every point onto u.

55
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:15,109
z = uᵀx′ — the shadow along u.

56
00:05:15,359 --> 00:05:23,203
Double u → projections double
→ Var ×4.

57
00:05:23,453 --> 00:05:29,335
That's cheating. So: uᵀu = 1.

58
00:05:29,585 --> 00:05:36,449
Try x: variance = 10/3 ≈ 3.33.

59
00:05:36,699 --> 00:05:40,621
Try y: the same.

60
00:05:40,871 --> 00:05:46,753
Try the diagonal:
variance = 6.

61
00:05:47,003 --> 00:05:53,867
What makes one direction
better than another?

62
00:05:54,117 --> 00:06:00,000
The one with greatest
projected variance.

63
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,793
The projected values: zᵢ = uᵀxᵢ′.

64
00:06:06,043 --> 00:06:16,665
Variance of the z's:
Σ(zᵢ − z̄)² / (n − 1).

65
00:06:16,915 --> 00:06:23,674
Centring ⇒ the z's
average to zero.

66
00:06:23,924 --> 00:06:31,649
So: Var(z) = Σzᵢ² / (n − 1).

67
00:06:31,899 --> 00:06:36,728
zᵢ² = (uᵀxᵢ′)(uᵀxᵢ′)
      = (uᵀxᵢ′)(xᵢ′ᵀu).

68
00:06:36,978 --> 00:06:42,771
A scalar equals its own transpose.

69
00:06:43,021 --> 00:06:44,953
Regroup: uᵀ(xᵢ′xᵢ′ᵀ)u.

70
00:06:45,203 --> 00:06:51,962
The middle term is
the outer product.

71
00:06:52,212 --> 00:06:59,937
Factor the fixed u's
out of the sum.

72
00:07:00,187 --> 00:07:06,946
The remaining sum is
the scatter matrix.

73
00:07:07,196 --> 00:07:12,990
Var(z) = uᵀCu.
The key formula.

74
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:19,999
uᵀSu differs only
by n − 1.

75
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,771
Now the problem is precise:

76
00:07:24,021 --> 00:07:28,548
max uᵀCu  s.t.  uᵀu = 1.

77
00:07:28,798 --> 00:07:33,324
u is the direction we choose.

78
00:07:33,574 --> 00:07:38,100
C holds spread and
joint variation.

79
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:42,877
uᵀCu is the variance
we're buying.

80
00:07:43,127 --> 00:07:47,653
uᵀu = 1 blocks
scaling cheats.

81
00:07:47,903 --> 00:07:53,938
Solve the constraint
with a multiplier — λ.

82
00:07:54,188 --> 00:07:59,469
L = uᵀCu − λ(uᵀu − 1).

83
00:07:59,719 --> 00:08:05,000
When the constraint holds,
that term vanishes.

84
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,770
Differentiate. But expand first.

85
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:21,946
C = [[a, b], [b, d]], u = [p, q].

86
00:08:22,196 --> 00:08:30,545
uᵀCu = ap² + 2bpq + dq².

87
00:08:30,795 --> 00:08:36,758
∂/∂p = 2ap + 2bq.

88
00:08:37,008 --> 00:08:42,971
∂/∂q = 2bp + 2dq.

89
00:08:43,221 --> 00:08:51,569
∇(uᵀCu) = 2Cu —
cross terms match.

90
00:08:51,819 --> 00:08:56,590
…because C is symmetric.

91
00:08:56,840 --> 00:09:02,803
Same trick: ∇(uᵀu) = 2u.

92
00:09:03,053 --> 00:09:16,172
∇L = 2Cu − 2λu.
At a max, ∇L = 0.

93
00:09:16,422 --> 00:09:19,999
Cu = λu.

94
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:27,361
Cu = λu — C stretches u,
never rotates it.

95
00:09:27,611 --> 00:09:32,763
That's an eigenvector.
λ is the eigenvalue.

96
00:09:33,013 --> 00:09:38,166
Our best directions are
eigenvectors of C.

97
00:09:38,416 --> 00:09:45,041
For a unit eigenvector:
uᵀCu = λ(uᵀu) = λ.

98
00:09:45,291 --> 00:09:50,444
The eigenvalue IS
the variance along it.

99
00:09:50,694 --> 00:09:55,847
Eigenvalues are non-negative —
C is PSD.

100
00:09:56,097 --> 00:10:00,513
Symmetric ⇒ eigenvectors
can be perpendicular.

101
00:10:00,763 --> 00:10:07,388
v₁ · v₂ = ½ − ½ = 0.

102
00:10:07,638 --> 00:10:15,000
S = 3C — same vectors,
eigenvalues 18 and 2.

103
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,735
det(C − λI) = 0.

104
00:10:17,985 --> 00:10:21,815
(10/3 − λ)² − (8/3)² = 0.

105
00:10:22,065 --> 00:10:25,347
λ₁ = 6,  λ₂ = 2/3.

106
00:10:25,597 --> 00:10:29,427
λ = 6:  v ∝ (1, 1).

107
00:10:29,677 --> 00:10:31,865
v₁ = (1/√2)(1, 1).

108
00:10:32,115 --> 00:10:35,945
λ = 2/3:  v ∝ (1, −1).

109
00:10:36,195 --> 00:10:38,384
v₂ = (1/√2)(1, −1).

110
00:10:38,634 --> 00:10:45,199
Any unit u = av₁ + bv₂,
a² + b² = 1.

111
00:10:45,449 --> 00:10:49,278
uᵀCu = 6a² + (2/3)b² ≤ 6.

112
00:10:49,528 --> 00:10:54,999
Max at b = 0 —
the long diagonal wins.

113
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,987
Data → centre → S → C.

114
00:11:00,237 --> 00:11:05,224
Project → Var = uᵀCu → max.

115
00:11:05,474 --> 00:11:09,749
Eigenvectors fall out.
Keep the largest.

116
00:11:09,999 --> 00:11:13,562
PC1 keeps 6/(20/3) = 90%.

117
00:11:13,812 --> 00:11:15,949
PC2 keeps 10%.

118
00:11:16,199 --> 00:11:21,899
Project onto PC1: a 1-D
sketch keeping 90%.

119
00:11:22,149 --> 00:11:27,137
The other 10% is discarded
on purpose.

120
00:11:27,387 --> 00:11:33,087
90% is this dataset —
not a law.

121
00:11:33,337 --> 00:11:36,187
Not an eigenvector trick.

122
00:11:36,437 --> 00:11:40,000
The variance problem
demands eigenvectors.

